ps, plus it knows the page by updating the nametable/attribute. Hmm, actually SMB's ScreenLeft_X_Pos is 0-255 and it never exceeds 255? No, SMB levels are 16 screens wide; the scroll is 0-255 within a nametable, and when it crosses 255 the game flips nametables and resets the scroll to 0? No, the PPU scroll is 0-255 repeating.\n\nActually, in SMB the level is stored as \"block\" data and the camera position is tracked as a \"screen\" index 0-15 plus a pixel offset 0-255. Hmm.\n\nOK, I'll change strategy for proving $006D. Instead of moving the camera, I can prove the page byte using the *enemy* slot? No, must be the player.\n\nWait, actually — maybe I can make the write work by writing the page and low byte to a location that's on-screen, and choose the values so that ONLY the page differs from a control write. Design:\n\nFrom a state with camera C=185 and player page P=1, low L=41 (level 297, screen 112):\n- Control write: $006D=1, $0086=41 → Mario screen x 112 (no move).\n- Test write: $006D=0, $0086=249 → level 249, screen 64. Mario moves left by 48px, page 1→0.\n\nHmm still confounded.\n\nBetter: choose `low` the same in both:\n- Control: $006D=1, $0086=100 → level 356, screen 171.\n- Test: $006D=0, $0086=100 → level 100, screen -85 → off screen. Reset. Bad.\n\nBecause camera 185, level x must be ≥185 to be on-screen. So page 0 requires low ≥185. Page 1 with the same low gives screen = low+256-185 = low+71, which for low≥185 is ≥256 → off-screen. So with the same low, one of the two pages is always off-screen. Fundamental.\n\nTherefore, to isolate the page by a visible move, I must also move the camera. So I need to be able to set the camera. Let me find out if writing $071C and/or $073F moves the view. Let me test: while paused, write $073F=100 and $071C=100, step 1, screenshot. If the background scrolls, I can control the camera.\n\nActually $073F is the value the NMI writes to $2005 each frame (from the mapping). But is $073F written by the scroll logic every