lue and observe Mario's *screen* position change while the camera is temporarily unable to follow — but he vanishes. Not good.\n\nAlternatively, prove $006D by watching it while writing $0086 near the boundary and letting the natural movement carry. Specifically:\n- Set $0086 = 250 (near page end) with page 0, camera will follow. Then hold right with play_input: $0086 will wrap to 0 and $006D will become 1. Screenshot after shows Mario at screen ~112 with $006D=1. That's a write-assisted proof: I wrote $0086=250 (Mario moved), and the ensuing wrap incremented $006D. Hmm, but $006D was set by the game, not by my write.\n\nI think the most defensible write proof for the page byte is:\n\"write_memory $006D = 1 together with $0086 = <value that puts the level position on-screen given the camera>\" — but as shown that's impossible. \n\nHmm hold on, actually maybe it IS possible if I *reduce* the camera first by writing the player's position leftward... no, the camera only moves right.\n\nWait, actually, let's reconsider: after Mario has run to camera 185, the camera can't go back. But what if I write the player's page to 0 with a low byte such that the level x is still ≥ camera? Impossible since level = low ≤ 255 and camera=185 → low in [185,255] works! E.g., page=0, low=200 → level 200, camera 185 → screen x = 15. On-screen! \n\nWait, I made an arithmetic error earlier. Let me redo: camera C=185 (page 0, so camera absolute = 185). Player page=0, low=200 → level 200. Screen = 200 - 185 = 15. On-screen! \n\nEarlier I said \"player page1 low L, camera C, writing page=0 gives level L, need L≥C\". With L=200 and C=185, L≥C. But the player's *natural* low when at page1 is L = C+112-256 = C-144 = 41, which is < C. But I don't have to keep the same low! I can set low=200 → level 200, which is ahead of camera 185 → on-screen at x=15. \n\nSo the plan works! From the post-run state (camera 185, page1, low41):\n- Write $006D=0, $0086=200 → level 200, camera 185, screen x=15. Mario should appear at the far left.\n- Control: write $006D=1, $0086=200 → level 456, screen = 271 → off-screen → reset.\n\nHmm, the test write (page1) is off-screen. But the proof direction: if I write page=0 low=200 and Mario appears at x=15 (level 200), while the pre-write state was page1 low41 (level 297) with Mario at x=112. Changing page 1→0 and low 41→200: net level change -97px. Mario moves from screen 112 to 15 (-97). That's consistent with both bytes mattering, but muddy.\n\nBetter isolation: \n- Control A: write $006D=1, $0086=200 (level 456) → off-screen (invalid).\nNot usable.\n\nHmm. To isolate the page, I need two writes with the same low but different page, both on-screen. As shown impossible because they differ by 256 > screen width. So a pure single-byte isolation is impossible. Any real proof must use two different low values.\n\nBut here's a clean logical proof using the *terrain*: The screen shows a fixed 256px window of the level starting at camera X. If the player's position were interpreted as only $0086 (page ignored), then writing $006D=0/$0086=200 with camera 185 would put Mario at screen x=15, standing on whatever terrain is at level 200 (which is near the level start, on flat ground). If instead the page were honored, level 200. Both same. Not isolating.\n\nOK, alternative isolation trick: keep the *camera* the same but show that a $006D write with the SAME $0086 changes the terrain under Mario... but both can't be on-screen.\n\nLet me just accept: I'll prove $006D by a write where I set the pair to a page-1 level coordinate that the camera can reach... The camera can't be written. Hmm.\n\nActually! Here's the clean isolation: I can write the *player's* position to two different absolute levels that are both within the camera window, one in page 0 and one in page 1 — but the camera window is at absolute [C, C+255]. If the camera were at absolute ~300 (page 1), then page 1 and page 0... no, page 0 is below 256 < 300.\n\nThe camera window spans