relevant/misleading. Let me restore a clean state and do a controlled test.\n\nSimpler idea to advance the camera: just hold right with jumps to survive, then check the camera. But survival is unreliable.\n\nAlternative: Maybe I can set the player's position beyond the goomba while the camera is at 0 by *bypassing* the reset — e.g., write the position while the game is paused, then instead of stepping, take a screenshot? The screen won't update without stepping (the OAM/scroll are from the last frame). Hmm.\n\nHmm, what about writing the position and then reading whether the game resets in 0 frames? Not useful.\n\nLet me reconsider whether the earlier \"reset\" was actually the game clamping the camera to follow! When I wrote page1 low200 (level 456, camera 0) and stepped 1 frame, list_sprites showed Mario at x=40 and $006D=0. If the game had scrolled the camera to 344 (page1) and Mario appeared at 112, list_sprites would show x=112. It showed 40. So it reset. Unless the game \"rewound\" the whole level.\n\nOK, let me try the direct approach of beating/avoiding the goomba with jumps, and see how far the camera gets. Script: hold right; jump at appropriate times. Let me first check where the goomba is: slot1 low $0087, page $006E.\n\nActually, simpler: I can *move the goomba away* by writing its slot, as I planned. Then walk right freely (no enemy for a while), camera advances. Let me:\n1. Restore to a clean start: load_state 0.\n2. Pause.\n3. Write goomba slot (page $006E=0, low $0087=0) to move it to level 0.\n4. Resume and hold right for ~400 frames; Mario should walk freely to level ~500+, camera ~400.\n5. Then pause and check $006D/$0086/$071C/$073F.\n\nBut other enemies: after the first goomba, there's a Koopa? In 1-1: first goomba at 352, then a group of two goombas around 700, then a pipe. At level ~500 he's safe. 400 frames * 1.5 = 600px → level ~640. Might hit the next goombas. Let me do 300 frames → level ~490.\n\nThen camera ~380 (page1). Then for the page proof I need camera ∈[200,255] and a page-0 low value ahead of it. Hmm, if camera is 380, page-0 values (<256) are behind → off-screen. So I need camera in [200,255]. So walk ~250-300 frames to get camera ~250? Earlier 240 frames got camera 185 and death. With the goomba removed, 300 frames → camera ~250 (page 0 low 250). Then Mario is at level ~360 (page1 low ~104), screen 112.\n\nThen write $006D=0, $0086=250 → level 250, camera 250 → screen 0. Valid! Mario moves from screen 112 to 0. \n\nAnd to isolate: before the write, level = page1*256+104 = 360. If the page were ignored, level would be 104 < camera 250 → off-screen. Since Mario is on-screen at 112, level must be 360 → the page byte is honored. After writing page0/low250, level=250, screen 0.\n\nThat's a decent proof. Let me also do a positive page write: after that, write $006D=1, $0086=104 → level 360, screen 110 → Mario back. Both visible. That shows the page byte toggling between two absolute positions 250 and 360 (differ by 110, not 256... hmm).\n\nTo show the 256 offset: with camera at 250, write $006D=1, $0086=0 → level 256, screen 6. And $006D=0, $0086=0 → level 0 → screen -250 → off. Hmm.\n\nLet me just do: camera ~250.\n- Write page1, low 0 → level 256, screen 6. Screenshot: Mario at x≈6 (right after page boundary; terrain = level 256).\n- Write page0, low 0 → level 0, screen -250 → off-screen → reset (bad).\nNot good.\n\nAlternatively, keep low=200 for both? page0 low200 → level 200 < camera250 → off. no.\n\nThe best page demonstration at camera C: \n- page0 works only if low ≥ C.\n- page1 works for low ∈ [0,255].\nSo choose camera C=200: page0 low=250 (screen 50) vs page1 low=250 (screen 306, off). Or page0 low=200 (screen 0) vs page1 low=200 (screen 256, off).\nAny same-low comparison fails. So compare different lows and rely on the argument.\n\nActually here's the decisive test: with camera C, write page1 low=L1 such that screen x is small, and page0 low=L0 such that screen x is also small, and