within page 1 — the player moved to screen 203 (off-center but on-screen) and the camera followed. So changes up to +255 within the current page are fine (screen ≤ 255). A full +256 keeps the same low, so screen +=256 → off-screen.\n\nBut what if I write $006D=1 (level +256) AND reduce $0086 by 256 (i.e., same low)? That's no change at all. \n\nThe only way to isolate: write $006D while $0086 is such that the player stays on-screen. E.g., from the mid-level state (page1 low9, camera 153), write $006D=1, $0086=200 → level 456 → screen 303 → off-screen (>255). Reset.\n\nOr write $006D=0, $0086=200 → level 200, camera 153 → screen 47 → on-screen. Page went 1→0. If the page were ignored, level=200 too (same low). So the write is visible either way — not isolating, BUT the combination with the pre-write state (which required page=1) makes the case.\n\nAlternatively write $006D=1, $0086=240 → level 496, screen 343 → off.\nOr write $006D=0, $0086=100 → level 100, screen -53 → off/reset.\n\nSo from camera 153, page0 values on-screen are low ∈ [153,255]. Choose low=153 → screen 0. So: write $006D=0, $0086=153 → level 153, screen 0. If the game ignored the page (i.e., level=153 anyway... same). Argh, page0/low is self-consistent regardless.\n\nThe isolation I want: write $006D=1 with low=153 → level 409, screen 256 → off. So page1 is off-screen while page0 is at screen 0 with the same low. That's the 256 offset! So:\n- Write $006D=0, $0086=153 → level 153, screen 0. Mario visible at far left.\n- Write $006D=1, $0086=153 → level 409, screen 256 → off-screen → reset.\nThe contrast demonstrates $006D adds 256. But the second write resets the level. \n\nI could do the reverse order to keep things clean: \n1. From the mid-level state, write $006D=1, $0086=153 → level 409, screen 256 → off → the game resets Mario to the level start (I can screenshot Mario gone/at start). Then\n2. load_state, write $006D=0, $0086=153 → level 153, camera 153 → screen 0 → Mario visible at far left. Screenshot.\nTogether they show the page byte's 256 offset. But this is convoluted.\n\nGiven the time, let me settle for the primary evidence and be transparent. I'll do:\n- $0086 write proof: done (screenshot Mario at 200).\n- $006D: natural wrap observation (strong) + a write test from mid-level showing that with the page byte the same low yields different absolute positions.\n\nActually, let me do ONE clean demonstration for $006D that is a positive write + screenshot:\nFrom the mid-level state (page1 low9, camera 153), write $006D=0, $0086=200 → step 2 → Mario at screen ≈ 200-153 = 47. Screenshot shows Mario on the left. This is a write of $006D (1→0) and $0086 (9→200), and Mario moved from screen 112 to 47. The page byte was part of the change. Then, to show the page specifically, write $006D=1, $0086=9 back → Mario returns to 112. Hmm.\n\nHonestly the natural wrap is the strongest proof for the page. Let me also capture a natural page-1 screenshot to include: after the 170-frame run, Mario is at page1 and the HUD is normal; if I now write $006D=1, $0086=0 (level 256), Mario appears at screen ~103 (256-153). And with $006D=0, $0086=0 (level 0) he'd be off. \n\nLet me do the decisive positive test for the page:\nMid-level state: camera ~153, Mario page1 low9.\nTest: write $0086=0, keep $006D=1 → level 256, screen = 256-153 = 103. Step 2. Screenshot: Mario at x≈103.\nInterpretation: I wrote $0086=0. If the page byte were not part of the position (level=0), Mario would be 153px left of the camera → off-screen. He appears at 103, meaning level=256 → the page byte $006D supplies the 256. This is a clean positive proof of $006D using a write to $0086=0 (and $006D unchanged at 1). The only written byte is $0086, but the result proves $006D's value is used. That's solid!\n\nEven better: keep $006D untouched and write only $0086=0. The result (Mario at 103) proves the page byte contributes. \n\nLet me do that:\n1. Restore state 0, run right 170 frames (page1 low9, camera1